<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Yasawi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1166</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Khoja Ahmed Yasawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Yasawi was a Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poet and teacher of the 12th century, based at the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkistan-city">Yasi</a>, now Turkistan, in southern Kazakhstan. His order, the Yasawiyya, and his Turkic devotional verse spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among the nomads of the steppe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> built the great mausoleum over his tomb in the late 14th century, now a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-yasawi.jpg" alt="Ahmad Yasawi" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cihangir ASHUROV · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmad_Yasawi_miniature.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
